#### Article 20

The spiritual field as referred to in Article 14 paragraph (2) letter f has the duties:
a. to provide spiritual guidance to victims;  
b. to coordinate with related parties; and c. to prepare reports and recommendations for follow-up services;

#### Article 21

The psychology field as referred to in Article 14 paragraph (2) letter g has the duties:
a. to provide psychological guidance to victims;  
b. to prepare recommendations for follow-up services; and c. to coordinate and refer to follow-up services

#### Article 22

1. PPT shall have at least 5 (five) counselors.  
2. Counselors as referred to in paragraph (1) comprise:a. counselors in the legal field;  
b. counselors in the health field;  
c. counselors in the psychology field;  
d. counselors in the social field; and e. counselors in the spiritual field.

#### Article 23

The organizational structure of PPT established by the Regent involves related SKPD with community institutions adjusted to needs.

#### Article 24

Further provisions regarding the development, mechanism and networked working relationship of PPT shall be further regulated by Regent Regulation.

# CHAPTER V

## FORMS AND MECHANISMS OF PROTECTION

## Part One

Prevention

#### Article 25

1. Prevention efforts against violence toward Children and Women shall be carried out in an integrated manner by the Regional Government coordinated by the agency having the principal duties and functions of coordination in the field of women's empowerment and child protection.  
2. Prevention efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented by:a. establishing a working network in violence prevention efforts and handling acts of violence through coordination, integration, synchronization of violence prevention based on partnership patterns;  
b. establishing a violence prevention system, mapping of locations or areas prone to acts of violence;  
c. organizing critical communication, information, and education about the rights of children and women for the community;  
d. conducting socialization about laws and regulations related to the protection of children and women; and e. prevention efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out in an integrated manner coordinated by the SKPD in charge of women's empowerment and child protection.

#### Article 26

Prevention efforts shall also be carried out by:
a. the community;  
b. the family; and c. parents.

## Part Two

Services

#### Article 27

1. The provision of services to victims shall be carried out in an integrated manner by PPT.  
2. PPT as referred to in paragraph (1) may receive and send case referrals from or to other service units in a networked manner.

#### Article 28

The provision of services to victims shall be implemented with: a. no fees charged;  
b. promptness;  
c. safety and comfort;  
d. empathy;  
e. non-discrimination;  
f. integration;  
g. easy accessibility; and h. guaranteed confidentiality.

#### Article 29

Forms of services to victims comprise:
a. complaint, consultation, and counseling services;  
b. assistance services;  
c. health services;  
d. social rehabilitation services;  
e. legal services;  
f. repatriation and social reintegration services; and g. psychology services.

#### Article 30

Complaint, consultation, and counseling services as referred to in Article 29 letter a comprise:
a. identification or initial recording of victims; and b. consent to take action.

#### Article 31

Assistance services as referred to in Article 29 letter b comprise:
a. accompanying victims during the examination and health recovery process;  
b. accompanying victims during the medicolegal process;  
c. accompanying victims during the examination process at the Police, Prosecutor's Office and court;  
d. monitoring the interests and rights of victims in the examination process at the Police, Prosecutor's Office and Court;  
e. maintaining the privacy and confidentiality of victims from all parties who have no interest, including reporting by mass media;  
f. coordinating with other assistants; and g. providing continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage.

#### Article 32

Health services as referred to in Article 29 letter c comprise: a. first aid to victims;  
b. treatment and recovery of physical injuries aimed at restoring the physical condition of victims carried out by medical and paramedical personnel; and c. referral to health services.

#### Article 33

Social rehabilitation services as referred to in Article 29 letter d constitute services provided by assistants in order to restore the traumatic condition of victims, including the provision of safe houses to protect victims from various threats and intimidation for victims and to provide social support so that victims have self-confidence, strength, and independence in resolving their problems, by:
a. providing spiritual guidance to victims; and b. psychological recovery of victims.

#### Article 34

Legal services as referred to in Article 29 letter e to assist victims in undergoing the judicial process by: a. providing legal consultation that includes information regarding the rights of victims and the judicial process;  
b. accompanying victims at the investigation, prosecution, and examination levels in court hearings and assisting victims to fully describe the violence they have experienced; and c. coordinating with fellow law enforcers, volunteer assistants, and social workers so that the judicial process proceeds as it should.

#### Article 35

1. Repatriation and social reintegration services as referred to in Article 29 letter f aim to return victims to their families and social environment.  
2. Repatriation and social reintegration services as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out by the Regional Government in coordination with: a. other Regional Governments; and b. related agencies and institutions both government and non-government.

#### Article 36

Psychology services as referred to in Article 29 letter g aim to:
a. provide psychological assistance and services; and b. provide psychological recovery of victims from trauma.

## Part Three

Empowerment

### Paragraph 1

Empowerment of Women Victims of Violence

#### Article 37

Forms of empowerment of women victims of violence comprise: a. job training, comprising:

1. skills training;  
2. field work practice; and
3. apprenticeship.  b. productive economic enterprises and joint business groups, comprising: 1. entrepreneurial skills training;  
2. facilitation of joint business group formation; and
3. business implementation assistance.  c. capital assistance, comprising:
1. work facilities and infrastructure assistance; and
2. facilitation of working capital assistance.

### Paragraph 2

Protection and Fulfillment of Children's Rights

#### Article 38

1. Protection for children in conflict with the law shall be implemented through:a. treatment of children in a humane manner in accordance with the dignity and rights of children;  
b. provision of special assistant officers for children from an early stage; c. provision of special facilities and infrastructure;  
d. imposition of appropriate sanctions for the best interests of children; e. continuous monitoring and recording of the development of children in conflict with the law.  f. provision of guarantees to maintain relationships with parents or family; and g. protection from identity reporting through mass media and to avoid labeling.  
2. Forms of fulfillment of the rights of child victims of violence, comprise:  a. rehabilitation efforts both within institutions and outside institutions; b. provision of safety guarantees for victim witnesses and expert witnesses whether physical, mental, or social; and c. provision of accessibility to obtain information regarding case development.  
3. PPT and the community are obligated to fulfill the rights of child victims of violence.
